J.R.Hey@bradford.ac.uk (JR HEY) wrote:

>Gene Irwin (girwin@frugal.com) wrote:

A lot of stuff that I agree with, but then:

>Also take a look at the Que reference books, which are damn fine reference
>manuals in general, with very good indexes. Not exactly hot on
>readability, but the best for thoroughness I have found if you want to
>find out something in a hurry. Their "Dos Programmers Reference" is
>compulsory for all people programming at anything other than the highest
>level in DOS. 

I've read a couple or three Que books. Two were unmemorable, but the
3rd was something like C++ By Example - it was 700 pages long AND
DIDN'T EXPLAIN WHAT THE SCOPE RESOLUTION OPERATOR MEANS!
C++ isn't an easy language, but a copy of Lipmann's C++ Primer and
Scott Meyer's Effective C++ would be a  big help to anyone trying to
learn the language.

For assembly, the *best* source is Randall Hyde's Art Of Assembly
(available as a 14MB download in zipped pdf, do an Alta Vista search.)
If you need a jump start before taking that down, Jeff Duntemann's
book published by Coriolis is best.

The best book on programming that I've ever read was Jon Bently's
Programming Pearls.

Don't forget to read up on fundamental algorithms and data structures
- knowing when you should use a binary tree and when a vector is
important as knowing the details of your chosen language.

Hope that was some use.

